WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:05.330
When Bird Box flew onto Netflix in late December,
the post-apocalyptic horror thriller quickly

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.100
became something of a phenomenon.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:12.220
All anyone on the Internet could talk, tweet,
and theorize about was Sandra Bullock's character

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:17.160
Malorie, her fight for survival in a world
where deadly entities spark global psychosis,

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.900
and how the planet tumbled into such terrifying
chaos.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.040
But the two biggest mysteries that are still
lingering in viewers' minds are what the creatures

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.940
look like and why the movie didn't actually
show them.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:38.140
Thanks to Bird Box screenwriter Eric Heisserer,
lead actress Bullock, and director Susanne

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.040
Bier, we now have answers to both those questions.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.280
Heisserer, Bullock, and Bier recently sat
down for an interview with Bloody Disgusting,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.789
and during the conversation, Heisserer revealed
that the film very nearly included a scene

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:52.119
in which the monsters are shown in their full
form.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.320
Heisserer explained:

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.680
"There was a time when one of the producers
was like, 'No, you have to see something at

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:02.449
some point' and forced me to write essentially
a nightmare sequence where Malorie experiences

00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:03.809
one in that house."

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:07.549
Bullock offered a detailed account of what
the monster looked like in the scene that

00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:10.160
didn't make it into the final cut of Bird
Box:

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.119
"It was a green man with a horrific baby face.

00:01:13.119 --> 00:01:18.030
It was snake-like, and I was like, 'I don't
want to see it when it first happens.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.229
Just bring it into the room.

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:20.120
We'll shoot the scene.'

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.500
I turn and he's like this [growling at me].

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:24.800
It's making me laugh.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.940
It was just a long fat baby."

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.660
After Bullock described the Bird Box monster,
director Bier chimed into the chat to explain

00:01:31.679 --> 00:01:35.799
that the creature's design was a little more
terrifying in theory than they were in practice,

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:39.880
and that unveiling to viewers the monsters'
appearance would take away their power.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.760
"It so easily becomes funny.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.710
We actually shot that [scene] and spent a
lot of energy on [it], but every time I saw

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.319
it, I was like, 'This is not going to be tense.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:49.620
It's just going to be funny."

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:54.080
Heisserer added that he was "so sorry" Bier
and Bullock had to shoot that sequence, but

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:58.950
with hindsight, everyone can laugh about the
situation and agree that the choice not to

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.460
show the monsters in Bird Box was the right
one.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.929
Bier went on to explain that the more the
team looked at the monsters, the more powerless

00:02:05.929 --> 00:02:06.929
they became.

00:02:06.929 --> 00:02:09.920
"Whatever those beings are, they tap into
your deepest fear.

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.860
Everybody's deepest fear is going to be different
from the other person.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.520
I think to suddenly take upon a concrete shape
in order to illustrate that becomes weak.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:23.040
Where the conceit is really strong, then trying
to illustrate it is kind of almost meaningless.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.840
So it would have been the wrong decision."

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.819
Opting to keep the monsters' appearance ambiguous,
only showing black shadows and wind gusts

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:34.260
to signal their impending arrival, was a smart
one, as it's often what we don't see that

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.420
scares us most.

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:47.080
However, as anyone who has seen Bird Box will
know, the film took the complete opposite

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:51.930
approach with its depiction of the violence
that the creatures cause, showing the self-killings

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.020
in great detail.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.680
Bier explained the thought process behind
the all-in directorial choice, noting that

00:02:57.689 --> 00:03:02.099
Netflix doesn't "have any inhibitions at all"
when it comes to such shocking sights and

00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:06.300
that the Bird Box team wanted everything to
feel as real as possible without becoming

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:07.480
unnecessarily gory.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.340
"I think we pretty much wanted to do it so
it felt real and at the time was harrowing.

00:03:12.349 --> 00:03:15.120
But not so that would become the essence of
it.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.730
It's pretty much done so it's kind of this
is what it would look like if they actually

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:20.960
did what they are doing.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.650
I think if you stick to that, you get it relatively
realistic."

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:29.280
Bird Box remains the talk of Twitter and the
movie on everyone's mind, and with countless

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.040
viewers comparing the film to John Krasinski's
breakthrough fright-fest A Quiet Place, it's

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.260
certainly aiding the renaissance of sense
horror.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.500
That the film has captivated so many people
without ever showing its main monsters is

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.740
perhaps an even bigger accomplishment.

